A||||3||burdell1||ENGINE CLEANING
Z||000000||burdell1||05-05-99||07:54 AM||||WHat is the best and safest way to clean my engine compartment and the front compartment too? Without having to worry about starting any electrical fires or getting water in the oil (if that's possible.)
Z||000001||Carrolles||05-05-99||11:11 AM||||I use Superclean.  You can get it at Walmart.  I take a piece of plastic drop cloth and cover the trunk.  I use bungee cords to strap the plastic to the lip of the trunk opening.<br>Spray superclean all over the engine and use a parts cleaning brush (or an old stiff paintbrush) to work on the heavy goop.  Let it sit for a few minutes and hose off.  Start the engine soon after to help speed up drying before water seeps into the electrical connectors too much.<br>I've been doing this for years and have never had a problem, even without covering the alternator.  <br>Good luck,<br>Carroll
Z||000002||theogre||05-05-99||02:33 PM||||water generally won't bother the alternator, but avoid getting cleaner in it. the front, pully end, of the alternator shaft uses a sealed ball bearing pack. the back end tho uses a simple sleeve bearing and you don't want cleaner to wash out the lubricant.<p>as long as the valve covers, PCV valve grommet, etc are in good shape, you shouldn't have water in the oil. make sure you have good plug wires tho or you could be doing some hand drying for a while before it starts. <p>I've used a number of methods to clean, from spray ons, to just preasure washing, never really had a problem with any method on a good motor. If you use a presure washer avoid directly spraying the PCV grommets, and oil cap. Those things have enough presure to drive water thru into the engine. A few drops of water in the engine won't hurt it, but a pressure washer that's used badly can flood it. Don't presure wash the TBI unit on 4 cyl cars.<p><br>I've found that after using some spray cleaners you still need to clean the dist cap, coil top(s), and sometimes the plug wires. I usually just spray them with glass cleaner, or 409, and wipe them off. that gets any engine cleaner residue off so it won't collect dust/moisture and cause hard starting. 
Z||000003||EMF||05-05-99||04:16 PM||||shouldnt have any trouble with the front compartment, i used soap and sprayed it out with a hose to clean mine the other day, just dont spray right on any relays or wire  harness's <p><br>
